────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
On the name, and on forks
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

You may call your fork RÚNA. The name is not reserved and no permission is
needed to keep it — a fork that renames everything is harder for people to
relate back to what it came from, which helps nobody.

What section 6 of the licence withholds is the right to use *Vardr Labs LLC*
to endorse or promote your version. Do not present a modified build as one we
produced, reviewed, or vouch for.

This matters more here than it would for most software. RÚNA is a security
tool: its value rests on claims about what the server can and cannot see, and
those claims were established by reading and testing a specific tree. A build
with the cryptography altered can be byte-for-byte plausible and completely
untrue. Section 4(b) of the licence therefore requires that modified files
carry prominent notices saying they were changed — please treat that as a
floor rather than a formality.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Verifying a build is the one we published
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Licence text cannot stop a dishonest build; evidence can. Every RÚNA server
answers /version with the git commit it was built from and the SHA-256 of the
web bundle it is serving:

    curl -s https://<host>/version

Compare both against the corresponding GitHub Release. Release artefacts are
signed with cosign (keyless, via Sigstore), so their provenance is checkable
without trusting us to be honest about it. If /version reports "dev", the
build carries no provenance at all and should not be trusted with anything
that matters.

